      "physicalAssumptions": "Closed classical system with constant total mass and material constituents in an inertial frame; Newton third-law pair cancellation. Positive total mass for weighted coordinate. Rigid body means fixed interpoint distances. Planar statics uses zero two-component resultant plus zero signed moment about the perpendicular axis. Ideal hinge reaction force passes through pole; no unassigned hinge couple. Lever drawing is schematic, and quantitative biomechanics additionally requires tendon direction and limb weight as source explicitly states.",
      "pedagogicalReview": "Weighted coordinates precede center-of-mass dynamics; rigid-body and lever discussion adds rotational equilibrium to translation. Defines perpendicular arm and signed moments, and explains a hinge as useful pole. Explicit limitations on tendon geometry and limb weight prevent treating the schematic as a full biomechanical numerical case. Reviewer adds pivot reaction in the illustrative balance; arrow lengths are not interpreted as force magnitudes.",
